+(duration-opts)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a string into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. Two forms are accepted:
+
+-   A whole number of seconds, such as `120` or `0`.
+-   One or more `<integer><unit>` groups using the units `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m` (minutes) and `s` (seconds).
+    Each unit may appear at most once, in the order `d`, `h`, `m`, `s`, with no spaces. Units are case-insensitive.
+    Examples: `2d`, `1h30m`, `45S`, `1d2h3m4s`, `90m`.
+
+Anything else, such as `-5`, `1.5h`, `1h 30m` or `30m1h`, is rejected. An existing `timedelta` is passed through unchanged.
+
+```python
+@click.command()
+@click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration())
+def run(timeout):
+    click.echo(timeout)
+```
